Close the glLineWidth half of the deferred-state gap, where it can matter.

Companion to gl4es-point-size-batch.patch. glLineWidth has the same shape of
bug as glPointSize did: the generated wrapper in wrap/gles.c sets the driver
state immediately, while immediate-mode geometry is batched into a renderlist
and drawn later, so a width set between primitives applies to the ones already
recorded. Display lists were already handled (PUSH_IF_COMPILING records the
call); only the immediate-mode batch was not.

Unlike point size, this one usually cannot be seen: GLES and WebGL stacks
commonly report GL_ALIASED_LINE_WIDTH_RANGE as [1, 1] and rasterize every line
one pixel wide whatever glLineWidth was set to. Measured under
ANGLE/SwiftShader via WebGL2: the range is [1, 1], and a scene drawing one line
at width 9 and another at width 1 produces two identical one-pixel lines. So an
unconditional flush here would break batching to fix pixels that the driver
cannot draw differently anyway.

Hence the gate. Record the widest line the driver admits once, in
GetHardwareExtensions (hardext.maxlinewidth, alongside the other max queries),
and flush the pending batch only when that is greater than 1 -- correct on a
stack with real wide lines, free on one without.

The early-out for a repeated width is the part that does something on every
target: gl4es_mirror_line_width (from gl4es-getter-client-state.patch) is
authoritative, since glPopAttrib and display-list replay both come back through
gl4es_glLineWidth and nothing else calls gles_glLineWidth directly. A no-op set
now costs nothing instead of a driver call -- worth having where every GL call
crosses into JS, and where an app resets glLineWidth(1.0f) after each pass.

Verified: web build renders pixel-identically to the previous build on a scene
carrying line loops, vertex outlines and point markers (0 differing pixels).

Applies after gl4es-getter-client-state.patch, whose mirror block this extends.
Applied by scripts/web-deps.sh after cloning the pinned gl4es checkout.
--- a/src/glx/hardext.h
+++ b/src/glx/hardext.h
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
     int maxlights;      // maximum number of light
     int maxsize;        // maximum texture size
     int maxplanes;      // maximum clip planes
+    int maxlinewidth;   // GL_ALIASED_LINE_WIDTH_RANGE upper bound (1 on stacks that cannot widen a line)
     int blendsub;       // GL_OES_blend_subtract
     int blendfunc;      // GL_OES_blend_func_separate
     int blendeq;        // GL_OES_blend_equation_separate
--- a/src/glx/hardext.c
+++ b/src/glx/hardext.c
@@ -391,6 +391,19 @@
     // Now get some max stuffs
     gles_glGetIntegerv(GL_MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE, &hardext.maxsize);
     SHUT_LOGD("Max texture size: %d\n", hardext.maxsize);
+    {
+        // Widest line the driver can actually rasterize. Several GLES/WebGL
+        // stacks report [1, 1] here, i.e. glLineWidth cannot change a pixel;
+        // glLineWidth uses this to skip work that would only matter where
+        // lines can be widened.
+        GLfloat linewidthrange[2] = {1.0f, 1.0f};
+        LOAD_GLES(glGetFloatv);
+        gles_glGetFloatv(GL_ALIASED_LINE_WIDTH_RANGE, linewidthrange);
+        if(gles_glGetError()!=GL_NO_ERROR || linewidthrange[1] < 1.0f)
+            linewidthrange[1] = 1.0f;
+        hardext.maxlinewidth = (int)linewidthrange[1];
+        SHUT_LOGD("Max line width: %d\n", hardext.maxlinewidth);
+    }
     gles_glGetIntegerv((hardext.esversion==1)?GL_MAX_TEXTURE_UNITS:GL_MAX_TEXTURE_IMAGE_UNITS, &hardext.maxtex);
     if (hardext.esversion==1) {
         gles_glGetIntegerv(GL_MAX_LIGHTS, &hardext.maxlights);
--- a/src/gl/wrap/gles.c
+++ b/src/gl/wrap/gles.c
@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@
 #include "gles.h"
 #include "../gl4es.h"
 #include "../loader.h"
+#include "../../glx/hardext.h"
 #include "skips.h"
 
 // emulation of 'glTexParameteri' (for internal use only)
@@ -1405,6 +1406,21 @@
 #endif
     {
         extern GLfloat gl4es_mirror_line_width;
+        // A repeated width is a no-op: nothing to flush, nothing to tell the
+        // driver. gl4es_mirror_line_width is authoritative (every path that
+        // changes the width, glPopAttrib and display-list replay included,
+        // comes back through here).
+        if (gl4es_mirror_line_width == width)
+            return;
+        // Same trap as glPointSize: immediate-mode geometry is batched and
+        // drawn later, so a width set between primitives would otherwise apply
+        // to the ones already recorded. Flush only where the driver can
+        // actually widen a line -- a stack reporting
+        // GL_ALIASED_LINE_WIDTH_RANGE [1, 1] (most GLES/WebGL, including
+        // ANGLE) renders every width identically, so the flush would break
+        // batching and change no pixel.
+        if (hardext.maxlinewidth > 1)
+            FLUSH_BEGINEND;
         gl4es_mirror_line_width = width;
     }
     gles_glLineWidth(width);
